The request line (or start line) is the first part of an HTTP request and tells the server what kind of request it’s dealing with. It has three main parts: the HTTP method, the URL path, and the HTTP version.

Example: METHOD /path HTTP/version

100-199= informational responses
    Server has received part of the request and it waiting for the rest
200-299= successful responses
    every worked as expected
300-399= redirection messages
    resource moved to a different location, usually provides the new URL
400-499= Client Error Responses
    Problem with the request
500-599= server error responses
    Sevrer encountered an error, there's server-side issues that are not the client's fault

Content Security Policy:
    Allows teh admin to set what resources and sources are safe to help prevent XSS attacks
HSTS (Strict Transport Security):  
    Helps ensure that web browsers will only connect over HTTPS
X Content Type Options:
    Instructs the browser to not guess the MIME type of a resource
Referrer Policy:
    This header controls the amount of information sent to the destination web server when a user is redirected from the source web server, such as when they click a hyperlink

